{% extends 'help/article.html.twig' %}

{% block body %}
    <p>
        Fleet Pro has one rule that shapes the setup order: <strong>records flow downhill from
        your equipment and people to your loads</strong>. A load needs a driver, a driver needs a
        truck, and maintenance needs an odometer. Set things up in that order and the app fills
        in most of the detail for you; do it backwards and you will type the same VIN three times.
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Connect your ELD, if you have one. It imports trucks and drivers for you.</li>
        <li>Fill in the gaps on those trucks and drivers, or add them by hand if you have no ELD.</li>
        <li>Add your trailers.</li>
        <li>Connect email and SMS so you can send loads out.</li>
        <li>Invite your team and set what each person can see.</li>
        <li>Build your first load.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Start with the ELD</h2>
    <p>
        If you run <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'integrations' }) }}">Quantum, TrackPro or MAX</a>,
        connect it before you type anything. The import pulls your trucks and drivers across with
        their VINs, unit numbers and licence details, and from then on it keeps odometer readings
        and GPS positions current by itself. That matters beyond convenience:
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'maintenance' }) }}">maintenance intervals</a> measured
        in miles are only as good as the mileage feeding them.
    </p>
    <p>
        The import always shows you what it is about to do — what it will create, what it will
        link to a record you already have, what it will refresh and what it will retire — before
        it changes anything. Read that screen the first time. It is also how you catch a truck
        that exists twice under two unit numbers.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
        <p><strong>No ELD?</strong> Nothing is locked. Add trucks and drivers by hand and type odometer
        readings when you record maintenance. You lose the live map and automatic mileage, and
        everything else works the same.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Fill in what the ELD cannot know</h2>
    <p>
        An ELD knows a truck's identity, not its paperwork. After the import, go through
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'trucks-and-trailers' }) }}">each truck</a> and add
        plate expiration, inspection dates and the service intervals you actually run. Do the same
        for <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'drivers' }) }}">drivers</a>: CDL expiration,
        medical card expiration and endorsements. Those fields are what later tell you a driver
        cannot take a hazmat load, so an empty one is not neutral — it is a load you will have to
        re-assign at the last minute.
    </p>

    <h2>Trailers</h2>
    <p>
        Trailers are not imported — no ELD reports them — so they are the one roster you type in
        full. The fields that earn their keep are type, payload capacity, and the equipment flags
        (E-track, Thermo King, hazmat certified). Those drive
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'assigning-drivers' }) }}">driver suggestions</a>,
        so a blank trailer record means a truck that never gets suggested for the freight it can
        actually haul.
    </p>

    <h2>Connect email and SMS</h2>
    <p>
        Sending a load to a driver goes out through <strong>your</strong> SendGrid account and
        <strong>your</strong> Twilio number, not ours, so your driver sees a message from your
        company and a reply reaches you. Both are set up in Settings → Integrations and both have
        a test button. Use it — finding out the API key is wrong while you are configuring it is
        far better than finding out when a driver says they never got the load.
    </p>

    <h2>Invite your team</h2>
    <p>
        Add people under Users &amp; Access. Everyone gets a role, and the role decides what they
        see — a dispatcher books loads without seeing profit, an operations manager runs the fleet
        without touching user accounts. You can grant or revoke any individual permission on top
        of the role, so you rarely need to compromise. See
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'users-and-permissions' }) }}">Users and permissions</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Build your first load</h2>
    <p>
        Now the setup pays off. On a new <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'loads' }) }}">load</a>
        you enter stops and freight details, pick a driver from a list that already knows who is
        eligible, and the truck and trailer come along with them. Add the rate, and the app has
        everything it needs to show you what the run made.
    </p>

    <div class="warn">
        <p><strong>One thing to know early:</strong> deleting a record does not erase it. Delete flips a
        status so history, old loads and the audit trail stay intact. That is deliberate, but it
        means a mistyped driver should be corrected rather than deleted and re-created.</p>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
